# Erdős Problem #99 — matched `g=0.0050` internal/nonbridge boundary dichotomy

**Date:** 2026-09-14  
**Target:** C-II, canonical full-core Type-II branch  
**Dependencies:** matched iterative-pruning gap `g=0.0050`; Section-171 `epsilon=0.0023374`; shallow-tooth internal penetration; bridge-deduplicated long-side concentration  
**Claim class:** proof-only source-level propagation awaiting independent audit  
**External literature:** no new import

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

The previous matched-gap propagation counted approximately `0.2D` distinct physical
contacts in either the tooth or long-side branch. Two independently hostile-audited
finite improvements remove both factor-two losses:

- a shallow two-edge tooth selects a distinct **internal unloaded-to-unloaded** contact,
  not merely a contact distinct up to endpoints;
- a selected outer occurrence is duplicated only by a graph bridge, and the full-core
  bridge-isoperimetric bound pays the entire outer-bridge population by half the
  metric detour.

At the matched `g=0.0050` endpoint this gives a branch-uniform physical-edge floor
above `0.3948D`.

Write

\[
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},\quad
s=\sqrt{\Delta_2},\quad
C_{146}={4\over9}(\pi+s),
\]

\[
g=0.0050,\qquad
t_{50}=C_{146}+0.0023374,
\qquad C_{50}=2s+g.
\]

The topology/detour remainder is

\[
R_{50}={9t_{50}\over2}-2\pi-C_{50}=0.00551830,
\]

so

\[
\boxed{d_{out}/D\le\delta_{50}:=0.00275915+o(1).}
\tag{IN.1}
\]

The exact outer-walk burden yields

\[
\max\{N_2,L_{>2}\}
\ge B_{50}D-o(D),
\]

with

\[
\boxed{B_{50}=0.4362350385367828447\ldots .}
\tag{IN.2}
\]

---

## 1. Tooth branch: edge-injective internal penetration

The fixed-scale Type-II flatness theorem proves that a two-edge tooth apex has no
loaded contact neighbor other than the two loaded endpoints of its own base.  Thus its
mandatory third contact is internal to one unloaded positive-contact component.

For every fixed

\[
0<\eta<2(1-\cos(\pi/12)),
\]

the inner endpoint of the selected contact lies more than `sqrt(3)/2` inside the
support line of the tooth base.  A shallow tooth apex lies less than `sin(pi/12)` from
its own nearby support line.  Type-II fixed-scale boundary flatness therefore prevents
the inner endpoint from being another shallow tooth apex.  The selection is
edge-injective:

\[
\boxed{
E_{int}(\eta)
\ge N_{sh}(\eta)
\ge N_2-{d_{out}\over\eta}.
}
\tag{IN.3}

Taking the exact convenient value `eta=1/15`, the matched constants give

\[
\boxed{
{E_{int}(1/15)\over D}
\ge B_{50}-15\delta_{50}-o(1)
=0.3948477885367828447\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{IN.4}

Every counted edge has both endpoints unloaded, lies inside one positive-contact
component, and has inward component greater than

\[
Y_{1/15}={29\sqrt3-\sqrt{59}\over60}
=0.7091387945271472\ldots .
\tag{IN.5}

If directional flux is the objective, `eta=0.06` gives

\[
{E_{int}(0.06)\over D}
\ge0.3902492052034495\ldots-o(1)
\tag{IN.6}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
{1\over D}\sum_{e\in E_{int}(0.06)}
\operatorname{proj}_{in}(e)
>0.2803910036960501-o(1).
}
\tag{IN.7}

The inner endpoint of every edge in this cohort is at absolute local depth greater
than `sqrt(3)/2` from its tooth-base support line.

---

## 2. Long-side branch: bridge-deduplicated nonbridge contacts

Let `B_o` be the number of graph bridges whose two sides are incident to the outer
face.  Full-core bounded-face area saturation and bridge-subtracted isoperimetry give

\[
\boxed{B_o\le d_{out}/2+o(D).}
\tag{IN.8}

For a direction threshold `beta`, the exact outer-route energy identity removes at
most

\[
{d_{out}\over1-\cos\beta}
\]

bad occurrences.  Discard every occurrence carried by an outer bridge.  There are at
most `2B_o<=d_out+o(D)` such occurrences, and every remaining outer occurrence is a
distinct physical nonbridge edge.  Hence

\[
\boxed{
E_{tan}^{nb}(\beta)
\ge L_{>2}
-\left({1\over1-\cos\beta}+1\right)d_{out}
-o(D).
}
\tag{IN.9}

Choose the tight direction window

\[
\beta=\pi/8.
\]

Then at the matched endpoint

\[
\boxed{
{E_{tan}^{nb}(\pi/8)\over D}
\ge0.3972287385779480\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{IN.10}

Each edge is a distinct nonbridge outer-face unit contact within `22.5` degrees of the
corresponding loaded-hull side.  Because it is not a bridge, its other side is a
bounded face.  Therefore every selected edge is physically one of:

1. a boundary/terminal edge of a strict unit-rhombus train; or
2. a boundary incidence of a bounded nonquadrilateral exceptional face.

Using the looser `beta=pi/6` window raises the count to

\[
E_{tan}^{nb}(\pi/6)/D
\ge0.4128813125653755\ldots-o(1).
\tag{IN.11}

---

## 3. Unified source-level endpoint

Every matched-gap low-`t` Type-II survivor therefore obeys

\[
\boxed{
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{either}&
\ge0.3948477885D-o(D)
\text{ distinct internal unloaded-to-unloaded penetration contacts},\\[1mm]
\text{or}&
\ge0.3972287385D-o(D)
\text{ distinct nonbridge outer contacts within }22.5^\circ
\text{ of local long-side tangents}.
\end{array}
}
\tag{IN.12}

In particular, regardless of branch,

\[
\boxed{0.3948477885D-o(D)}
\]

distinct actual unit-contact edges lie in one of two strongly localized source
families.  This is essentially a factor-two improvement over the first `g=0.0050`
physical propagation.

The two families now land in ledgers with no occurrence ambiguity:

- tooth edges are internal edges `e_r` of unloaded components and have a fixed-depth
  endpoint;
- long-side edges are nonbridge outer edges and split canonically into rhombic-train
  terminals versus exceptional-face incidences.

---

## 4. Remaining obstruction

The scarcity problem is no longer the issue.  Both branches force roughly `0.4D`
**distinct** physical contacts.  What is still missing is a weight-free conversion of
one of these populations into a strict source-level descent.

The next useful targets are correspondingly specific:

1. component capacity: bound how many fixed-depth internal penetration edges one
   harmonic component can absorb per unit attachment/diameter currency;
2. phase/interface capacity: charge the nonbridge near-tangent cohort to rhombic train
   terminals or exceptional-face boundary incidences without losing a linear factor;
3. simultaneous deformation: extract a linear independent subfamily whose contact
   constraints can be preserved while every active diameter derivative is made
   nonpositive.

No positive KKT-weight floor is claimed.  C-II and Level I remain open.

## 5. Scope audit

- The internal-penetration theorem and its hostile audit use Type-II fixed-scale
  near-disk flatness, C26, triangle-freeness, and harmonic trapping.
- The bridge theorem uses full-core bounded-face area saturation; it is not a local
  harmonic-cell assertion.
- The `g=0.0050` atomic theorem and Section-171 propagation are independently audited.
- All displayed populations count distinct physical contact edges, not route
  occurrences.
- Contact-isolated source points remain in the source.
